        <div id="do-teens-need-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Do Teens Need Protein Powder? Usually Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the numbers, because they surprise most families. The US Dietary Reference Intakes set the protein RDA at 34 g a day for children aged 9 to 13, 46 g a day for girls aged 14 to 18 and 52 g a day for boys aged 14 to 18. Per kilogram of body weight, that works out to roughly 0.95 g for younger children and 0.85 g for older teens. A teenager who eats eggs at breakfast, a sandwich with meat or cheese at lunch, a glass of milk and a normal dinner with chicken, fish, beans or tofu is very likely already above those targets, and national survey data have long shown that average protein intakes among American teens comfortably exceed the RDA.
          </p>

          <p>
            Teen athletes need more than sedentary teens, but not as much more as supplement marketing implies. The American Academy of Pediatrics' guidance for young athletes notes that while athletes have higher protein needs, they can usually meet them with a balanced diet, and that even the higher intakes some young athletes may need are readily reached with a typical mixed diet. What a growing athlete is more often short on is total calories: two-a-day practices, a growth spurt and a skipped breakfast can leave them underfueled, which no protein shake fixes on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the main reason pediatric sports medicine groups lean toward food first. Food delivers protein alongside the carbohydrate a teen needs for training, the calcium and vitamin D for bones that are still building density, iron for growing blood volume, and fiber and micronutrients that powders lack. It is also cheaper. A glass of milk, a carton of Greek yogurt or a peanut butter sandwich after practice does most of what a scoop of powder does, for a fraction of the price.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes protein powder harmful in sensible amounts for a healthy teenager. It makes it optional. The rest of this guide is about the situations where it is genuinely useful, and how to choose one so that the convenience does not come with contaminants, stimulants or banned substances. For a broader look at daily targets by goal, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">guide to how much protein you need</a> covers adults, and the principles carry over.
          </p>

