        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked: Certification and Nothing Extra
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these products or run taste panels with teenagers. The picks come from label analysis, publicly listed third-party certifications and published guidance from pediatric and sports nutrition bodies, the method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Comments on taste and mixing reflect what buyers commonly report.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification was the first and hardest filter. Every powder on the list carries NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice status according to the brand or certifier. NSF Certified for Sport screens for more than 270 substances banned in sport, verifies that the label matches what is in the tub and, according to NSF, checks for harmful levels of contaminants such as heavy metals. Informed Choice tests batches for banned substances and audits manufacturing. For a teenager, that matters for two reasons: supplements in the US are not approved before sale, and contamination with stimulants or steroid-like compounds has been found repeatedly in products sold as muscle builders.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second filter was what is not in the product. We excluded anything containing caffeine or other stimulants, creatine, so-called testosterone boosters, fat burners, nitric oxide or pump blends, and proprietary blends that list ingredients without amounts. Plenty of excellent adult products include some of these; none of them belongs in a teenager's daily shake. We also skipped mass gainers, whose 600 to 1,200 calorie servings are rarely appropriate without a pediatrician or dietitian involved.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only then did we weigh protein per serving, tolerance, taste and price. Most picks are whey, because it is a complete protein that is easy to digest and, as a group, dairy proteins have tested lower for lead than plant proteins in independent testing. We included one certified plant protein for vegetarian, vegan and dairy-allergic teens, one isolate for sensitive stomachs, a budget option and a ready-to-drink shake for the bag.
          </p>

