        <div id="is-it-safe" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Protein Powder Safe for Teenagers?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a healthy teenager, the protein in a powder is not the safety issue. Whey, casein, pea and soy protein are foods, and one serving a day sits well within normal intakes. Worries that protein damages healthy kidneys are not supported by the evidence at sensible intakes, though teens with kidney disease or other medical conditions should only use supplements under their doctor's direction. The real concerns are what else can end up in the tub and what the shake displaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contamination is the first concern. In October 2025, Consumer Reports tested 23 protein powders and shakes and reported that more than two-thirds contained more lead in a serving than its experts' conservative daily level of concern, with plant-based products averaging about nine times the lead of dairy-based ones. Separately, government and academic testing has found products sold as muscle-building supplements that contained undeclared stimulants, steroid-like compounds or prescription drug ingredients. Third-party certification does not remove every risk, but it is the best filter available to a parent standing in a supplement aisle. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains what testing covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Displacement is the second. A teen who drinks a shake instead of eating breakfast or lunch can miss out on carbohydrate for training and on iron, calcium, vitamin D and fiber, which matter a great deal during growth. Teen girls in particular often fall short on iron and calcium, and a protein shake in water provides little of either. The fix is simple: use a shake alongside meals, not instead of them, and mix it with milk or blend it with fruit when you can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Body image is the third, and the one parents most often miss. Researchers have linked heavy use of muscle-building supplements in adolescents with body dissatisfaction, muscle dysmorphia and disordered eating, particularly in boys, who are less often screened for these problems. A teen who is anxious about missing a shake, counts protein grams obsessively, trains through injury or talks constantly about getting bigger or leaner deserves a gentle conversation and, if needed, a referral from their pediatrician. Powder is not the cause, but it can be part of the picture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="red-flags" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Red Flags: Stimulants, Creatine and Testosterone Boosters
          </h2>

          <p>
            The supplement aisle markets to teenage boys in particular, and many of the products next to protein powder are a different category entirely. Pre-workout powders often contain 200 to 300 mg or more of caffeine per scoop, sometimes with other stimulants, which is more than many teens should have in a whole day. The American Academy of Pediatrics has long advised that energy drinks have no place in the diets of children and adolescents, and stimulant-heavy pre-workouts raise the same concerns about heart rate, blood pressure, sleep and anxiety.
          </p>

          <p>
            Creatine is the most researched sports supplement in adults, but the picture for teenagers is different. The AAP's clinical report on performance-enhancing substances groups creatine with other performance enhancers and recommends that pediatricians discourage their use by adolescents, and many creatine labels themselves state that the product is not intended for people under 18. Some researchers argue it is likely safe for older teens, but long-term data in growing athletes are limited, and the AAP report also notes that early use of over-the-counter performance supplements may be associated with later use of anabolic steroids. Creatine decisions belong with a pediatrician or sports medicine physician, not a store clerk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Testosterone boosters, so-called natural anabolics and anything mentioning hormones, prohormones or SARMs should be off the table entirely. At best, the herbal versions do little; at worst, some products sold this way have been found to contain actual steroids or unapproved drugs that can affect growth, mood and fertility and trigger failed drug tests. Fat burners, appetite suppressants and detox products fall into the same bucket for teenagers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical check is quick. Turn the tub around. If the ingredient list contains anything other than protein, sweetener, flavor, thickener, lecithin and perhaps added vitamins, ask why it is there. If you see a proprietary blend, caffeine, green tea or guarana extract, creatine, beta-alanine, yohimbe, tribulus or words like anabolic, test or shred, choose something else. Plain protein is boring, and for a teenager boring is exactly right.
          </p>

