        <div id="drug-testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Drug-Tested Athletes: Why the Certification Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many high school associations, college programs and youth national teams drug-test athletes, and anti-doping rules generally work on strict liability: the athlete is responsible for whatever is in their body, whether or not they knew a supplement was contaminated. A positive test caused by a tainted protein powder can still cost an eligibility season or a scholarship. For any teen who competes, or hopes to compete, at a level where testing happens, certification is not a nice extra but the baseline.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport is the program most widely recognized by professional leagues and many college athletic departments, and it is the safest default for a teen who might be tested. Informed Choice and its sister program Informed Sport are also well established and test batches against banned-substance lists. All of these certifiers acknowledge that screening reduces risk rather than eliminating it, and US anti-doping authorities make the same point.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two habits make certification meaningful. First, verify the exact product and flavor in the certifier's online database rather than trusting a seal on a website, because certification can apply to some flavors or sizes and not others. Second, keep the tub or a photo of the lot number until it is finished, so there is a record if a question ever comes up. Buying from the brand or a major retailer rather than third-party marketplace sellers reduces the risk of counterfeit or tampered products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coaches and athletic trainers can help here, and many college programs have sports dietitians who maintain approved supplement lists. If your teen is being recruited or already competes at a high level, ask the program what they use. For a list of certified options aimed at adult competitors, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-athletes/">athletes guide</a>, and for products ranked first on contaminant screening, our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What About Protein Powder for Kids?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For younger children, the case for protein powder is weaker still. The protein RDA is 19 g a day for children aged 4 to 8 and 34 g a day for ages 9 to 13, amounts that a cup of milk, an egg and a small portion of meat, beans or tofu largely cover. A single scoop of adult powder delivers 20 to 25 g, which can be most or all of a young child's daily requirement in one drink, displacing foods that provide the energy, iron, calcium and fiber children need to grow.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are worried that a child is not eating enough protein, or is not growing as expected, the first step is the pediatrician, not the supplement aisle. They can track growth on a chart, look for underlying problems and, if needed, refer you to a pediatric registered dietitian. Children who need nutritional support are sometimes advised to use pediatric nutrition drinks, which are formulated for children's needs and are a different category from adult protein powders. Use them on your pediatrician's advice rather than as a routine snack.
          </p>

          <p>
            For picky eaters, small food-based strategies usually work better than powders: adding milk to oats, offering yogurt, cheese, eggs, hummus or peanut butter in familiar forms, and serving a protein food the child already accepts at each meal. If a pediatrician does suggest adding a little protein powder to food, an unflavored, certified product with no added sweeteners, herbs or stimulants is the most predictable choice, and the amount should be what they recommend, not a full adult scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same red flags apply with even more force at younger ages: no caffeine, no creatine, no herbal blends, no weight-loss products and nothing marketed with muscle or body-shape claims. If a child or young teen is asking for protein powder because of how they want their body to look, that conversation is often more important than the purchase. For family-friendly ways to add protein to food, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">smoothie guide</a> and <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">baking guide</a> show how unflavored powders work in recipes for older teens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying from the supplement aisle's loudest shelf</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Products marketed to teenage boys often bundle protein with caffeine, creatine, test boosters or proprietary blends. None of those belong in a teen's shake, and some carry a real risk of contamination with banned substances. Buy plain protein with an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice listing, and verify it in the certifier database.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing meals with shakes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A scoop in water provides protein but almost none of the carbohydrate, iron, calcium, vitamin D and fiber a growing athlete needs. Teens who swap breakfast or lunch for a shake often end up underfueled for training. Use a shake alongside meals, mixed with milk or blended with fruit, never as the meal itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming more protein builds more muscle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Once a teen meets their protein needs, extra protein does not add muscle. Strength comes from puberty, consistent supervised training, enough calories and sleep. Two or three shakes a day mostly add cost and displace food. One scoop is plenty for nearly every teen who uses powder at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a seal without checking the database</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Certification can apply to some flavors and sizes but not others, and counterfeit products exist on third-party marketplaces. Look up the exact product in the NSF or Informed Choice database, buy from the brand or a major retailer, and keep the lot number until the tub is finished, especially for drug-tested athletes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing signs of body-image stress</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heavy supplement use in adolescents has been linked with body dissatisfaction and disordered eating, including in boys. Obsessive protein counting, anxiety about missed shakes, or constant talk about getting bigger or leaner deserve a calm conversation and, if needed, a referral from the pediatrician. The powder is rarely the real issue.
              </p>
            </div>
