        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is protein powder safe for teenagers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a healthy teen, one serving a day of a plain, third-party certified protein powder is generally considered safe as a supplement to food. The risks are contamination, hidden stimulants or banned substances in poorly regulated products, and shakes replacing meals. Choose NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice products, and check with a pediatrician if your teen has a medical condition.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best protein powder for teens?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An independently certified, plain protein with nothing extra. Klean Athlete Klean Isolate and Thorne Whey Protein Isolate are NSF Certified for Sport, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is a lower-cost Informed Choice option, and Garden of Life Sport is the certified plant-based pick. Avoid anything with caffeine, creatine or testosterone-booster claims.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein does a teenager need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The RDA is 46 g a day for girls and 52 g a day for boys aged 14 to 18, or about 0.85 g per kg of body weight. Teen athletes may need roughly 1.2 to 1.6 g per kg, sometimes more during heavy training, which most reach through regular meals. A pediatrician or dietitian can personalize the number.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">At what age can you start taking protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no official minimum age, because protein powder is a food product, but pediatric guidance favors food first for children and teens. Most teens do not need it. For older teen athletes with genuine gaps, one serving of a certified product is reasonable. For younger children, use protein powder only if a pediatrician recommends it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can protein powder stunt growth or hurt kidneys?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no good evidence that plain protein powder stunts growth, and normal intakes do not harm healthy kidneys. Growth problems are more likely when shakes displace meals and a teen eats too few total calories. Teens with kidney disease or other conditions should only use supplements under medical supervision. Steroid-contaminated products are a separate, serious risk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should teens take creatine with their protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends that pediatricians discourage performance-enhancing substances, including creatine, in adolescents, and many creatine labels say they are not for people under 18. Long-term data in growing athletes are limited. If your teen is interested, discuss it with a pediatrician or sports medicine physician rather than buying it alongside protein.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is protein powder okay for kids?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually it is unnecessary. Children aged 4 to 8 need about 19 g of protein a day and those aged 9 to 13 about 34 g, which normal meals cover. One adult scoop can supply most of a young child's daily need and displace other foods. Talk to your pediatrician before giving a child protein powder or a nutrition drink.
              </p>
            </div>

