          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most teenagers do not need protein powder. Regular meals with eggs, dairy, meat, fish, beans or tofu cover the RDA and usually the higher needs of teen athletes too. A shake earns its place when schedules, a plant-based diet or a small appetite leave a genuine gap, and even then one serving a day alongside meals is plenty.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you buy one, buy plain, certified protein: Klean Isolate or Thorne for drug-tested athletes, Gold Standard on a budget, Garden of Life Sport for plant-based teens. Walk past anything with caffeine, creatine, boosters or fat burners, and involve a pediatrician or registered dietitian for younger children, medical conditions or any sign of body-image stress.
